{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae8fd9d9a083d978", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tA Modular Part-of-Speech Tagger for Scottish\n2\tGaelic using spaCy\n3\tPéter Stefán, Peter J. Barclay, and Alistair Lawson\n4\t\n5\tarXiv:2608.04808v1 [cs.CL] 5 Aug 2026\n6\t\n7\tSchool of Computing, Engineering, & the Built Environment,\n8\tEdinburgh Napier University,\n9\t10 Colinton Rd, Edinburgh, Scotland, UK\n10\t\n11\tAbstract. Part-of-speech tagging for low-resource languages remains\n12\tchallenging due to limited annotated data, especially for linguistically\n13\tcomplex languages. Gàidhlig (Scottish Gaelic) is a morphologically rich\n14\tand endangered language with limited digital resources, making it suitable for examining a lightweight language processing approach. This\n15\tpaper describes using the modular spaCy Natural Language Processing framework to build part-of-speech taggers for Gàidhlig using the\n16\tAnnotated Reference Corpus of Scottish Gaelic. We train two models\n17\twith minimal pre-processing and configuration: one using a fine-grained\n18\ttagset and another using a reduced coarse-grained tagset. Both models\n19\tare trained without external embeddings or pre-trained language models,\n20\tusing only supervised learning from the available corpus. The fine-grained\n21\tmodel achieves 88.6% tagging accuracy, while the coarse-grained model\n22\tachieves 93.7%. The results are comparable to those of the two previously\n23\tpublished Gàidhlig taggers, indicating that simple, off-the-shelf language\n24\tprocessing pipelines can demonstrate good performance in low-resource\n25\tand morphologically complex linguistic settings.\n26\tKeywords: low-resource languages; part-of-speech tagging; Gàidhlig;\n27\tspaCy\n28\t\n29\t1\n30\t\n31\tIntroduction\n32\t\n33\tRecent advances in Natural Language Processing (NLP), particularly the rise\n34\tof large language models (LLMs), have demonstrated a strong dependence on\n35\tlarge amounts of training data as well as requiring expensive computational\n36\tresources. A correlation can be found between larger training corpora and the\n37\teffectiveness of the models [12]. However, this data-driven approach poses an\n38\tissue for low-resource languages, where annotated corpora are limited in quantity\n39\tand availability.\n40\tGàidhlig (Scottish Gaelic) is such a low-resource language. Despite research\n41\tinterest and ongoing language revitalisation efforts, existing datasets and NLP\n42\ttools for Gàidhlig remain limited. Available annotated corpora, such as the Annotated Reference Corpus of Scottish Gaelic (ARCOSG), are relatively smaller\n43\tthan those for high-resource languages. This limits the development of robust\n44\tstatistical or neural language models.\n45\t\n46\t\f2\n47\t\n48\tPéter Stefán et al.\n49\t\n50\tIn addition, Gàidhlig presents further challenges owing to its rich morphological structure. Grammatical information is often represented by the use of\n51\tinflections and word form mutations, leading to a large variety of surface forms\n52\tfor a given lemma [26]. Part-of-speech (PoS) tagging and other NLP tasks are\n53\tparticularly difficult with morphologically complex languages, especially in a\n54\tlow-resource setting [13]. As a result, low-resource NLP systems often focus on\n55\tapproaches leveraging detailed linguistic knowledge, using complex architectures\n56\tand specialised feature engineering.\n57\tThis paper investigates whether a lightweight, general-purpose NLP framework can facilitate the construction of PoS taggers for Gàidhlig and low-resource\n58\tlanguages in general. Specifically, we explore the use of the spaCy library [9] to\n59\ttrain two tagger models directly on the ARCOSG corpus with minimal configuration or preprocessing employed. Two levels of tag granularity are tested, aiming\n60\tto assess the trade-off between simplicity and performance in a low-resource\n61\tmorphologically complex setting.\n62\tThe main contribution of this study is a reproducible lightweight Gàidhlig\n63\tPoS tagging architecture, using off-the-shelf NLP tools and compatible with the\n64\tspaCy software ecosystem, providing a competitive baseline for future research\n65\tin Gàidhlig and other low-resource NLP settings. In contrast to the dozens of\n66\tvaried PoS tagging systems available for widely used languages such as English\n67\t(see [4] for a survey), to our knowledge only two taggers have been previously\n68\tdescribed for Gàidhlig (see Section 2.2). We now provide now a third tagger, the\n69\tfirst to use spaCy, making our code publicly available.\n70\t\n71\t2\n72\t\n73\tBackground\n74\t\n75\tNatural Language Processing is a very important part of human computer interactions. NLP techniques are used in everyday situations, such as spell checkers\n76\tand speech recognition tools. The study of n-grams and statistical language\n77\tmodelling represented a major advancement in NLP: text corpora can be used\n78\tto observe patterns and establish word sequence probabilities, which then can\n79\tbe used to model the internal logic of a given language [3].\n80\t2.1\n81\t\n82\tGàidhlig\n83\t\n84\tGàidhlig is an Insular Celtic language indigenous to the British Isles, primarily\n85\tspoken in the Scottish Highlands and Western Isles. Despite its widespread former use, and its great historical and cultural significance, the number of speakers\n86\thas declined in recent centuries, owing to a combination of historical suppression and broader political and social processes that have favoured the widespread\n87\tadoption of English, resulting in its classification as an endangered language [11].\n88\tMany revitalisation efforts have been made in response, including the development of digital and NLP-based tools in recent years. These could prove crucial\n89\tin supporting language learning, improving accessibility and representation in\n90\tonline spaces, and importantly helping the digitisation of Gàidhlig [20]. A brief\n91\t\n92\t\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n93\t\n94\t3\n95\t\n96\toverview of the history and current status of Gàidhlig can be found in a recent\n97\tstudy by Barclay [23].\n98\tGàidhlig presents several key challenges in the development of NLP tools,\n99\ttypical of many low-resource languages. The existing corpora are very limited\n100\tin size and availability. One primary and invaluable resource is the Annotated\n101\tReference Corpus of Scottish Gaelic (ARCOSG), described in [17]. In contrast\n102\tto high resource languages, Gàidhlig has a much smaller digital presence overall,\n103\tmaking the development of robust NLP tools significantly more difficult [15].\n104\tIn addition, Gàidhlig is a morphologically complex language. Grammatically\n105\timportant information is encoded into word inflections, mutational processes,\n106\tand syntactic constructions [16,24]. NLP tasks are more difficult in such languages due to the added complexity of finding the lemma of words, which is\n107\toften an important first processing step [28]. Additionally, morphologically rich\n108\tlanguages have a much higher variety in their surface word forms, meaning that\n109\ta single lexical ‘word’ will have many variant forms occurring in texts [26]. The\n110\tincreased variety will make each individual form less likely to occur in any given\n111\ttraining corpus, resulting in a higher number of out of vocabulary (OOV) words\n112\t– words that appear in a given text that have not been encountered before.\n113\tThus, a language model might have more difficulty generalising effectively [13].\n114\tThis problem is amplified in low-resource contexts owing to the already limited occurrences of these variant forms in the more restricted annotated corpora\n115\t[13,22].\n116\t2.2\n117\t\n118\tRelated work\n119\t\n120\tDespite these challenges, in recent years efforts have been made to develop computational resources for Gàidhlig. These include the creation of a dependency\n121\ttreebank [1], which focused on capturing the morphological complexity and key\n122\tgrammatical features of Gàidhlig. Projects on PoS taggers, linguistic analysis,\n123\tand speech recognition applications have contributed to expanding the range\n124\tof digital tools available for the language [19,2,7,14]. However, many existing\n125\tlow-resource NLP systems remain highly specialised, typically designed for a\n126\tsingle task. They rely on custom architectures with rule-based components or\n127\tlanguage-specific feature-engineering to achieve high results.\n128\tThe first tagger available for Gàidhlig, described by Lamb et al. [18], uses\n129\tan ensemble-based Brill model, incorporating both stochastic and rule-based\n130\tmethods, organised around a backoff mechanism. Following statistical tagging,\n131\tcorrective rules are applied to increase the accuracy of the final tags. The only\n132\tother prior tagger for Gàidhlig, developed by Boizou et al. [2] as part of the\n133\tGaelic Linguistic Analyser (GLA) project, is based on the scikit-learn toolkit\n134\tand uses additional training data in addition to ARCOSG. This tagger uses\n135\tadditional features to capture grammatical structure, including prefixes and suffixes, sentence position and collocated word forms. Although both systems are\n136\teffective, approaches requiring explicit linguistic expertise and language-specific\n137\tfeature-engineering can increase system complexity and require greater development effort while reducing adaptability.\n138\t\n139\t\f4\n140\t\n141\tPéter Stefán et al.\n142\t\n143\tThese issues reflect a common pattern in low-resource NLP, where the lack of\n144\tlarge annotated corpora leads to reliance on heavily engineered pipelines, transfer learning and multilingual pre-training approaches [8]. Such approaches can\n145\tintroduce considerable complexity and dependence on external resources, and\n146\tmay introduce a bias towards structures appearing any high-resource language\n147\tmaterials used. In the case of Gàidhlig, this can be detrimental due to structural\n148\tand morphological differences between the languages. For example, when using\n149\ttransfer learning, the characteristic Verb-Subject-Object (VSO) word order of\n150\tGàidhlig may not be adequately reflected in NLP resources developed primarily\n151\tfor SVO (Subject-Verb-Object) languages such as English.\n152\tTaking a new approach, this study investigates using a modern, lightweight,\n153\tgeneral-purpose NLP pipeline using spaCy, trained directly on the ARCOSG\n154\tcorpus with minimal pre-processing and configuration. This allows us to investigate how off-the-shelf NLP tools can be adopted for Gàidhlig PoS tagging. Our\n155\tstudy establishes a baseline for comparison with more specialised systems, with\n156\tthe main advantages being the ease of implementation and reduced system complexity. These characteristics make the approach potentially transferable across\n157\tother low-resource languages.\n158\t\n159\t3\n160\t\n161\tAim\n162\t\n163\tDespite recent progress, Gàidhlig NLP remains relatively underdeveloped and\n164\tis constrained by the limited annotated data and tools available. This paper\n165\texplores the effectiveness of Gàidhlig PoS tagging in a low-resource setting, focusing on tools previously unexplored in Gàidhlig research. We propose the use\n166\tof the spaCy architecture for efficient model development, with minimal preprocessing, and limited hyperparameter tuning. The aim is to investigate whether\n167\tsuch a lightweight NLP architecture can provide a useful baseline for future\n168\tdevelopment in Gàidhlig language processing.\n169\t\n170\t4\n171\t\n172\tMethodology\n173\t\n174\tExisting work on Gàidhlig PoS tagging has focused on statistical taggers, developing specialised models with curated resources [20,2]. While these contributions\n175\thave significantly advanced the computational resources available for Gàidhlig,\n176\tthey may be characterised as custom-built systems. Here, by contrast, we evaluate a general-purpose PoS tagging framework using off-the shelf components.\n177\tLeveraging the modern NLP toolkit spaCy [9] remains unexplored for Gàidhlig.\n178\tThis leaves open the question of whether lightweight, general-purpose pipelines\n179\tcould be used as a baseline system for Gàidhlig PoS tagging and other downstream NLP tasks.\n180\tTo address this gap, this study presents the first evaluation of a spaCy-based\n181\tpart-of-speech tagging pipeline for Gàidhlig. We propose two models trained\n182\ton the ARCOSG corpus with different levels of PoS tag granularity for wider\n183\tevaluation. Both are implemented with very limited tuning, aiming to assess\n184\t\n185\t\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n186\t\n187\t5\n188\t\n189\tthe baseline performance of lightweight models for low-resource PoS tagging.\n190\tThe results are reproducible, providing a useful basis for future Gàidhlig NLP\n191\tresearch.\n192\t4.1\n193\t\n194\tspaCy and the Pipeline Architecture\n195\t\n196\tAs the core NLP framework, we used spaCy [9], an open-source library for\n197\tindustrial-strength NLP. The library provides a modular pipeline architecture\n198\tthat supports a tokeniser, parser, tagger and other components for further downstream NLP tasks. The creators of spaCy also provide a number of pre-trained\n199\tmodels and pipelines, mostly for high-resource languages. Here, spaCy was selected owing to its modular architecture and existing NLP utilities, which reduced and simplified implementation, while allowing for language-specific customisation. Rather than requiring individual implementations of tokenisation,\n200\tfeature extraction, training, inference, and evaluation, these are provided through\n201\ta cohesive framework. The proposed pipeline is illustrated in Figure 1. Consequently, the implementation mainly focused on configuring an existing framework, assembling pipeline components and preparing language specific resources,\n202\trather than developing task-specific algorithms from scratch. This lowers the\n203\ttechnical barrier for future Gàidhlig NLP research and simplifying the development of additional language technologies.\n204\tThis approach is highly adaptable, so future work can build upon and extend\n205\tthe proposed system requiring only the development of additional components,\n206\tsuch as a lemmatiser, dependency parser or other language tools. This enables\n207\tfuture Gàidhlig NLP research to build incrementally upon the proposed system,\n208\tinstead of developing independent task-specific implementations, avoiding the\n209\tneed to redesign the overall architecture for each new project.\n210\t\n211\tFig. 1. Overview of the Gàidhlig PoS tagging pipeline.\n212\t\n213\tMost importantly, the spaCy framework provides a lang module containing\n214\tlanguage-specific information for the tokeniser. Thanks to the recent work of\n215\tresearchers at Cornell College [5], the Gàidhlig module now includes a list of stopwords and tokeniser exceptions. The tokeniser also uses a rule set shared across\n216\tmost languages as a default in spaCy1 . In addition to these built-in resources, the\n217\tproposed pipeline used some auxiliary rules for punctuation handling required\n218\tto ensure compatibility with the annotation conventions used in ARCOSG.\n219\t1\n220\t\n221\tGàidhlig language folder for spaCy: https://github.com/explosion/spaCy/tree/\n222\tmaster/spacy/lang/gd\n223\t\n224\t\f6\n225\t\n226\tPéter Stefán et al.\n227\t\n228\tThe pipeline was designed to be lightweight to reflect the low-resource conditions of Gàidhlig. Computationally expensive architectures, such as transformerbased models were excluded from consideration, since they typically require\n229\tmuch larger training corpora for effective performance. In contrast, this study\n230\tfocuses on a compact supervised pipeline that can be trained from the annotated ARCOSG corpus, with minimal pre-processing and limited computational\n231\toverhead, while still capturing the rich morphology of Gàidhlig.\n232\tThe proposed pipeline comprises three main stages: tokenisation of the raw\n233\ttext, contextual feature extraction using Tok2Vec, and PoS prediction using\n234\tspaCy’s tagger component. Each stage is described below.\n235\tTok2Vec2 is the first trainable component of the pipeline. It converts each\n236\tword from the raw input text into a numerical representation which captures\n237\tboth the word itself and its surrounding context. These representation are based\n238\ton features such as word shape and the normalised form (a standardised form\n239\tof the word used by spaCy, similar to the lemma). This helps the model encode\n240\timportant morphological information. The representations are then used as input\n241\tby the tagger, which predicts the most likely PoS label for each token.\n242\tThis architecture of spaCy is well suited to the morphological complexity\n243\tof Gàidhlig. Grammatical information is learned from subword structures, such\n244\tas affixes and other orthographic features, during the training process. This\n245\tallows the model to generalise to previously unseen tokens – with low-resource\n246\tlanguages, it is common to encounter words and surface forms that did not\n247\tappear in the training data [22]. PoS predictions are also heavily affected by\n248\tsyntactic context, making the contextual token representations from the Tok2Vec\n249\tcomponent especially important in such low-resource settings, where the corpus\n250\tmay otherwise contain insufficient examples [8].\n251\tThe context encoding component only considers local contextual information.\n252\tThis design prioritises vocabulary interactions nearer to the token, where PoS tag\n253\tdistinctions are resolved through the immediate context and the morphological\n254\tcues discussed above. As a result, the model learns through the relationship\n255\tbetween context and morphology, rather than the meaning of the entire sentence\n256\tor text. This also reduces model complexity, making the approach more suitable\n257\tfor the limited amount of training data available.\n258\tWe use no pre-trained vectors or external embeddings; the model must rely\n259\tpurely on gold-standard supervised labels from the annotated corpus.\n260\tGiven the limited training data, this lightweight pipeline architecture focuses\n261\ton local contextual and morphological representations during training. This helps\n262\tefficient learning in a low-resource context, while maintaining attention on orthographic and syntactic indicators that are relevant for PoS tagging [10].\n263\t\n264\t2\n265\t\n266\tTok2Vec documentation is available on the spaCy website: https://spacy.io/api/\n267\ttok2vec\n268\t\n269\t\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n270\t\n271\t4.2\n272\t\n273\t7\n274\t\n275\tData\n276\t\n277\tThe entire ARCOSG corpus [17] was used for training and evaluation in this\n278\tstudy. An 80-10-10 split was applied for training, validation and testing. Following the approach of prior studies [20] and [2], we use both a fine-grained (242 or\n279\t246 items) and a coarse-grained (40 or 41 items) tag set for training and evaluation, making the comparison between previous work and our proposed models\n280\tsimpler. The coarse-grained tags simplify some linguistic distinctions by grouping together a set of similar fine-grained tags. We opted for using all available\n281\tdata to maximise the training and test sets; since no pre-training is done, the\n282\tmodel is solely trained through supervised learning on the annotated corpus.\n283\t4.3\n284\t\n285\tPreprocessing\n286\t\n287\tConsistent with the aim of the study, pre-processing was intentionally kept minimal in order to evaluate the effectiveness of a lightweight NLP pipeline using a\n288\tspaCy-based structure in a low-resource context. No stemming or linguistic simplification was applied, to retain as much morphological information as possible,\n289\twhich is essential for morphologically complex PoS tagging [26,13].\n290\tThe preprocessing focused on tokenisation consistency and compatibility with\n291\tthe ARCOSG annotation system, while preserving orthographic and morphological structure. This minimal pre-processing was intended to keep the experiments\n292\treproducible and reduce over-dependence on language-specific modelling.\n293\t\n294\t5\n295\t\n296\tResults\n297\t\n298\tBoth tagging models were evaluated using a 10% randomly selected sample from\n299\tARCOSG (the unseen test data set). The main metric used to measure performance is tagging accuracy, meaning the percentage of PoS tags that were\n300\tcorrectly predicted by the model. The model using the larger ARCOSG tagset\n301\tachieved an accuracy of 88.60%. The other one, trained on the reduced set of\n302\ttags, performed with 93.7% accuracy. These results are comparable with the accuracy of previously reported Gàidhlig PoS taggers. Table 1 presents the model\n303\tarchitecture of both models and their performance.\n304\t\n305\tTagset Type\n306\tTagset Size\n307\tTraining Tokens\n308\tVocabulary Size\n309\tPOS Tag Accuracy\n310\t\n311\tFull Tagset Model\n312\tFine-grained PoS tags\n313\t215\n314\t70,890\n315\t7160\n316\t88.60%\n317\t\n318\tReduced Tagset Model\n319\tCoarse PoS tags\n320\t40\n321\t69,522\n322\t7114\n323\t93.70%\n324\t\n325\tTable 1. Performance and training set comparison between the full and reduced ARCOSG tagset models.\n326\t\n327\t\f8\n328\t\n329\tPéter Stefán et al.\n330\t\n331\tTwo models were evaluated, using the coarse- and the fine-grained tagset\n332\trespectively. The full tagset has 215 tags, the reduced set 40 coarse-grained\n333\ttags. The training corpus contains 70,890 total words, with a vocabulary of 7160\n334\tunique entries; this is slightly smaller for the reduced set. The spaCy architecture\n335\teven gives a warning when the training set is validated, indicating that the\n336\tcurrent number of examples may not be sufficient for proper training.\n337\tSome minor tokenisation inconsistencies remained, as ∼0.4% of the tokens are\n338\tlabelled “misaligned”. However, this was expected, as only the stop words and\n339\tbasic tokenisation exceptions were applied from the Gàidhlig language defaults\n340\tin the spaCy framework.\n341\t\n342\t6\n343\t\n344\tDiscussion\n345\t\n346\tBoth models show encouraging performance, with the full fine-grained model\n347\tachieving results comparable to earlier specialised systems of Lamb et al. [19]\n348\tand Boizou et al. [2]. The results in Table 2 suggest that a lightweight spaCybased pipeline can achieve competitive performance in low-resource settings for\n349\tmorphologically complex languages with minimal language-specific engineering.\n350\tThe difference in performance between the full and reduced tagsets highlights\n351\tthe morphological complexity of Gàidhlig, as the full tagset requires the model to\n352\tdistinguish between much finer grammatical categories. The reduced set collapses\n353\tlinguistically subtle and context dependent distinctions making the classification\n354\ttask substantially simpler. In the low-resource setting used in these experiments,\n355\tmany of the 215 fine-grained tags appear infrequently: this further increases the\n356\tdifficulty of this task. Without pre-training or external word embeddings, the\n357\tmodel must solely rely on corpus examples, which limits its ability to learn the\n358\tmore complex PoS tags.\n359\t\n360\tStudy\n361\t\n362\tTagset Type\n363\t\n364\tTagset Size\n365\t\n366\tDanso & Lamb (2014) Fine-grained 242 tags (PAROLE tagset)\n367\tLamb et al. (2016)\n368\tFine-grained 246 tags\n369\tLamb et al. (2016)\n370\tCoarse-grained 41 tags\n371\tBoizou & Lamb (2020) Fine-grained 246 tags\n372\tBoizou & Lamb (2020) Coarse-grained 41 tags\n373\tOur model\n374\tFine-grained 246 tags\n375\tOur model\n376\tCoarse-grained 40 tags\n377\t\n378\tAccuracy\n379\t76.6\n380\t84%\n381\t92%\n382\t90.7%\n383\t94.7%\n384\t88.6%\n385\t93.7%\n386\t\n387\tTable 2. Performance comparison of previous and proposed models. Lamb et al.[19]\n388\tprovided additional evaluation on the same model developed by Danso & Lamb[6].\n389\t\n390\tThese findings are significant given the simplicity of the implementation and\n391\tthe model architecture. The application of the spaCy framework appears to\n392\tperform effectively with minimal configuration. The models are able to learn\n393\t\n394\t\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n395\t\n396\t9\n397\t\n398\tmorphological patterns, suggesting that the Tok2Vec architecture can generalise\n399\treasonably well in low-resource settings through limited local context, subword\n400\tand orthographic information.\n401\tThe design decision to preserve morphological information during preprocessing is also supported by these results. As noted, no stemming or lemmatisation was applied, allowing the model to learn directly from the various surface\n402\tforms. The relatively strong performance, even with the full tagset, could suggest that preserving the surface features is just as beneficial as more customised\n403\tapproaches commonly used in low-resource tagging tasks.\n404\tThese experiments were designed to be reproducible, as the default settings\n405\twere applied in most cases. A future study could investigate customisation of\n406\tthe implementation, to investigate the best pipeline structure for Gàidhlig using\n407\tspaCy.\n408\tOur results suggest that a lightweight, general-purpose NLP pipeline can\n409\tprovide a practical and reproducible baseline for Gàidhlig PoS tagging. The\n410\tuse of spaCy significantly reduced the implementation difficulty of the proposed\n411\tpipelines. The use of two sets of tags with differing granularity highlights the\n412\timportance of tokenisation quality when addressing the complex grammatical\n413\tstructures in Gàidhlig.\n414\tFor Gàidhlig and other languages with complex morphology and limited resources, these results are promising for future experiments with similar pipelines\n415\tusing the spaCy architecture.\n416\t\n417\t7\n418\t\n419\tFurther Work\n420\t\n421\tAs this research is ongoing, we hope to implement similar pipelines using spaCy\n422\twith more sophisticated architectures. Ideas include introducing pre-training via\n423\tmultilingual models such as XLM-RoBERTA3 . This would allow evaluation of\n424\twhether multilingual models provide a better starting state. Some models, such\n425\tas XLM-RoBERTA, also include Gàidhlig texts in their training data, perhaps\n426\tmaking them a better choice. On the other hand, this approach could also introduce other limitations, such as a bias towards the grammatical structure\n427\tof high-resource languages in the training set leading to negative transfer [27].\n428\tFuture work could also investigate whether using cross-lingual transfer would\n429\tprovide any improvements for Gàidhlig models over the lightweight architecture\n430\tproposed here.\n431\tUsing additional data sources for unsupervised learning could be employed\n432\tto increase the learning potential of future models. Given the limited available\n433\tannotated corpora, this approach provides a more direct way of addressing the\n434\tissue. Unlabelled Gàidhlig texts could be used for self-supervised pre-training,\n435\tproviding the models with further examples to learn lexical and morphological\n436\tpatterns before tuning them with the annotated data. Data augmentation techniques, such as synthetic sentence generation, paraphrasing, morphology-aware\n437\t3\n438\t\n439\thttps://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model doc/xlm-roberta\n440\t\n441\t\f10\n442\t\n443\tPéter Stefán et al.\n444\t\n445\ttransformations, and synonym replacement, could also increase the diversity in\n446\tthe training set. These approaches, in the case of a language with complex morphology, may lead to improved generalisation [25].\n447\tAdditional analysis of the model predictions could pinpoint which tags are\n448\tthe most frequently misclassified and which grammatical distinctions are the\n449\tmost challenging. The significant performance difference shown between the full\n450\tand reduced tagsets suggests that most errors may occur because of fine-grained\n451\thighly specialised grammatical categories. Detailed error analysis could provide\n452\tvaluable insight into whether errors primarily arise between related fine-grained\n453\tcategories or broader syntactic ones. Understanding the confusion patterns of\n454\tthe models could guide future improvements in annotation schemes, model architectures, and preprocessing strategies [21].\n455\t\n456\t8\n457\t\n458\tConclusion\n459\t\n460\tThis paper presents our evaluation of the effectiveness of lightweight PoS tagging pipelines based on the spaCy architecture for Gàidhlig using the ARCOSG\n461\tcorpus. The goal was to assess whether a model with minimal preprocessing and\n462\tconfiguration could perform as well as the more heavily engineered approaches\n463\tcommon in low-resource NLP.\n464\tThe results show that both models achieved relatively strong performance.\n465\tThe first model using the fine-grained 215 tagset achieved 88.6%, while the\n466\treduced coarse-grained 40-tag model had an accuracy of 93.7%. These indicate\n467\tthat the proposed pipeline using the spaCy presets in most cases is still capable\n468\tof learning complex morphological and contextual representations for Gàidhlig\n469\ttags, despite the limited data available for training. The difference in accuracy\n470\tbetween the two models is due to the variation in tag granularity, highlighting\n471\tthe need for additional information to address subtle grammatical distinctions.\n472\tOverall, this work contributes a reproducible baseline for Gàidhlig PoS tagging, using the spaCy architecture with minimal configuration needed, which is a\n473\tnew approach for the language. The results are promising as they are comparable\n474\tin accuracy to previous work reported in the literature, which employed more\n475\tcomplex architectures and specific configuration for Gàidhlig grammatical structures. As new linguistic resources become available for Gàidhlig, baseline systems\n476\tsuch as ours can provide a foundation for future research into more advanced\n477\tNLP solutions and downstream language technologies to support the digital\n478\tpresence of this living, culturally important but under-resourced language.\n479\t\n480\tCode Availability Statement\n481\tAll code from this research is made publicly available in the GitHub repository at:\n482\thttps://github.com/Speter011/A-Modular-Language-Model-for-Scottish-Gaelic.\n483\t\n484\t\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n485\t\n486\t11\n487\t\n488\tReferences\n489\t1. Colin Batchelor. Universal dependencies for Scottish Gaelic: syntax. In Proceedings\n490\tof the Celtic Language Technology Workshop, pages 7–15, 2019.\n491\t2. Loı̈c Boizou and William Lamb. An online linguistic analyser for Scottish Gaelic.\n492\tIn Human Language Technologies – The Baltic Perspective, pages 119–122. IOS\n493\tPress, 2020.\n494\t3. Ciprian Chelba. Statistical Language Modeling, pages 74–104. Wiley-Blackwell,\n495\tJohn Wiley & Sons Ltd, John Wiley & Sons Ltd, The Atrium, Southern Gate,\n496\tChichester, West Sussex, PO19 8SQ United Kingdom, 2010. Parts of this chapter\n497\tappeared in Computer Speech & Language, vol. 14, no. 4, pages 283-332, October\n498\t2000, and are used with the permission of Elsevier Limited.\n499\t4. Alebachew Chiche and Betselot Yitagesu. Part of speech tagging: a systematic\n500\treview of deep learning and machine learning approaches. Journal of Big Data,\n501\t9(1):10, 2022.\n502\t5. Scottish Gaelic Collab. Gaelic lemmatizer (spaCy-compatible NLP pipeline). https:\n503\t//github.com/Scottish-Gaelic-Collab/lemmatizer gaelic, 2025. Accessed: 2026-0611.\n504\t6. Samuel Danso and William Lamb. Developing an automatic part-of-speech tagger\n505\tfor Scottish Gaelic. In John Judge, Teresa Lynn, Monica Ward, and Oisı́n Ruairı́\n506\tÓ Brian, editors, Proceedings of the Celtic Technology Workshop (CLTW 2014):\n507\tA Workshop of the 25th International Conference on Computational Linguistics\n508\t(COLING 2014), pages 1–5, Dublin, Ireland, August 2014.\n509\t7. Lucy Evans, William Lamb, Mark Sinclair, and Beatrice Alex. Developing automatic speech recognition for Scottish Gaelic. In Proceedings of the 4th Celtic\n510\tlanguage technology workshop within LREC2022, pages 110–120, 2022.\n511\t8. Michael A. Hedderich, Lukas Lange, Heike Adel, Jannik Strötgen, and Dietrich Klakow. A survey on recent approaches for natural language processing in\n512\tlow-resource scenarios. In Kristina Toutanova, Anna Rumshisky, Luke Zettlemoyer, Dilek Hakkani-Tur, Iz Beltagy, Steven Bethard, Ryan Cotterell, Tanmoy\n513\tChakraborty, and Yichao Zhou, editors, Proceedings of the 2021 Conference of the\n514\tNorth American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies, pages 2545–2568, Online, June 2021. Association for\n515\tComputational Linguistics.\n516\t9. Matthew Honnibal, Ines Montani, Sofie Van Landeghem, and Adriane Boyd.\n517\tspaCy: Industrial-strength natural language processing in Python, 2020. https:\n518\t//doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1212303.\n519\t10. Daniel Jurafsky and James H. Martin. Speech and Language Processing: An Introduction to Natural Language Processing, Computational Linguistics, and Speech\n520\tRecognition, with Language Models. Stanford University (online manuscript released January 6, 2026), 3rd edition, 2026. https://web.stanford.edu/∼jurafsky/\n521\tslp3/.\n522\t11. Anne Kandler, Roman Unger, and James Steele. Language shift, bilingualism and\n523\tthe future of Britain’s Celtic languages. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal\n524\tSociety B: Biological Sciences, 365(1559):3855–3864, 2010.\n525\t12. Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, Tom Henighan, Tom B. Brown, Benjamin Chess,\n526\tRewon Child, Scott Gray, Alec Radford, Jeffrey Wu, and Dario Amodei. Scaling\n527\tlaws for neural language models, 2020. https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361.\n528\t13. Irakli Kardava. A morphology-driven approach to NLP for a low-resource, highly\n529\tcomplex language. Vietnam Journal of Computer Science, 1:19, 2025.\n530\t\n531\t\f12\n532\t\n533\tPéter Stefán et al.\n534\t\n535\t14. Ondřej Klejch, William Lamb, and Peter Bell. A practitioner’s guide to building ASR models for low-resource languages: A case study on Scottish Gaelic. In\n536\tProceedings of Interspeech 2025, pages 728–732, 2025.\n537\t15. András Kornai. Language death in the digital age. Language, 1(8), 2012.\n538\t16. William Lamb. Scottish Gaelic: A comprehensive grammar. Routledge, 2024.\n539\t17. William Lamb, Sharon Arbuthnot, Susanna Naismith, and Samuel Danso. Annotated reference corpus of Scottish Gaelic (ARCOSG), 1997–2020, 2020. https:\n540\t//github.com/Gaelic-Algorithmic-Research-Group/ARCOSG.\n541\t18. William Lamb and Samuel Danso. Developing an automatic part-of-speech tagger for Scottish Gaelic. In Proceedings of the First Celtic Language Technology\n542\tWorkshop, pages 1–5, 2014.\n543\t19. William Lamb, Samuel Danso, and Alistair Lawson. Evaluating a Gaelic Part-ofSpeech Tagger and Reference Corpus. Technical report, University of Edinburgh,\n544\tEdinburgh, Scotland, 2016.\n545\t20. William Lamb and Mark Sinclair. Developing Word Embedding Models for Scottish Gaelic. In Actes de la conférence conjointe JEP-TALN-RECITAL, volume 6,\n546\tpages 31–41, 2016.\n547\t21. Kaihong Liu, Wendy Chapman, Rebecca Hwa, and Rebecca S Crowley. Heuristic\n548\tsample selection to minimize reference standard training set for a part-of-speech\n549\ttagger. Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 14(5):641–650,\n550\t2007.\n551\t22. Alexandre Magueresse, Vincent Carles, and Evan Heetderks. Low-resource languages: A review of past work and future challenges, 2020. https://arxiv.org/abs/\n552\t2006.07264.\n553\t23. Peter J Barclay. A Rule-Based Computational Model for Gàidhlig Morphology. In\n554\tProceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence, volume 1, pages 683–693. SCITEPRESS – Science and Technology Publications, Lda., March 2026.\n555\t24. Susan Ross. The Standardisation of Scottish Gaelic Orthography 1750–2007. PhD\n556\tthesis, University of Glasgow, 2016. https://theses.gla.ac.uk/7403/.\n557\t25. Gözde Gül Şahin and Mark Steedman. Data augmentation via dependency tree\n558\tmorphing for low-resource languages. In Proceedings of the 2018 Conference on\n559\tEmpirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 5004–5009, 2018.\n560\t26. Reut Tsarfaty, Djamé Seddah, Sandra Kübler, and Joakim Nivre. Parsing morphologically rich languages: Introduction to the special issue. Computational linguistics, 39(1):15–22, 2013.\n561\t27. Zirui Wang, Zachary C. Lipton, and Yulia Tsvetkov. On negative interference in\n562\tmultilingual models: Findings and a meta-learning treatment. In Bonnie Webber,\n563\tTrevor Cohn, Yulan He, and Yang Liu, editors, Proceedings of the 2020 Conference\n564\ton Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP), pages 4438–4450.\n565\tAssociation for Computational Linguistics, November 2020. https://aclanthology.\n566\torg/2020.emnlp-main.359/.\n567\t28. Marion Weller-Di Marco and Alexander Fraser. Analyzing the understanding of\n568\tmorphologically complex words in large language models. In Proceedings of the\n569\t2024 Joint International Conference on Computational Linguistics, Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC-COLING 2024), pages 1009–1020, 2024.\n570\t\n571\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3e0e77fc-8e17-4426-afd1-6639c0da2a51", "uuid": "66aa902a-024d-4829-83fe-34a4d6b7df50", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:58:30.260Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/gaelic_pos.txt", "content": "A Modular Part-of-Speech Tagger for Scottish\nGaelic using spaCy\nPéter Stefán, Peter J. Barclay, and Alistair Lawson\n\narXiv:2608.04808v1 [cs.CL] 5 Aug 2026\n\nSchool of Computing, Engineering, & the Built Environment,\nEdinburgh Napier University,\n10 Colinton Rd, Edinburgh, Scotland, UK\n\nAbstract. Part-of-speech tagging for low-resource languages remains\nchallenging due to limited annotated data, especially for linguistically\ncomplex languages. Gàidhlig (Scottish Gaelic) is a morphologically rich\nand endangered language with limited digital resources, making it suitable for examining a lightweight language processing approach. This\npaper describes using the modular spaCy Natural Language Processing framework to build part-of-speech taggers for Gàidhlig using the\nAnnotated Reference Corpus of Scottish Gaelic. We train two models\nwith minimal pre-processing and configuration: one using a fine-grained\ntagset and another using a reduced coarse-grained tagset. Both models\nare trained without external embeddings or pre-trained language models,\nusing only supervised learning from the available corpus. The fine-grained\nmodel achieves 88.6% tagging accuracy, while the coarse-grained model\nachieves 93.7%. The results are comparable to those of the two previously\npublished Gàidhlig taggers, indicating that simple, off-the-shelf language\nprocessing pipelines can demonstrate good performance in low-resource\nand morphologically complex linguistic settings.\nKeywords: low-resource languages; part-of-speech tagging; Gàidhlig;\nspaCy\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nRecent advances in Natural Language Processing (NLP), particularly the rise\nof large language models (LLMs), have demonstrated a strong dependence on\nlarge amounts of training data as well as requiring expensive computational\nresources. A correlation can be found between larger training corpora and the\neffectiveness of the models [12]. However, this data-driven approach poses an\nissue for low-resource languages, where annotated corpora are limited in quantity\nand availability.\nGàidhlig (Scottish Gaelic) is such a low-resource language. Despite research\ninterest and ongoing language revitalisation efforts, existing datasets and NLP\ntools for Gàidhlig remain limited. Available annotated corpora, such as the Annotated Reference Corpus of Scottish Gaelic (ARCOSG), are relatively smaller\nthan those for high-resource languages. This limits the development of robust\nstatistical or neural language models.\n\n\f2\n\nPéter Stefán et al.\n\nIn addition, Gàidhlig presents further challenges owing to its rich morphological structure. Grammatical information is often represented by the use of\ninflections and word form mutations, leading to a large variety of surface forms\nfor a given lemma [26]. Part-of-speech (PoS) tagging and other NLP tasks are\nparticularly difficult with morphologically complex languages, especially in a\nlow-resource setting [13]. As a result, low-resource NLP systems often focus on\napproaches leveraging detailed linguistic knowledge, using complex architectures\nand specialised feature engineering.\nThis paper investigates whether a lightweight, general-purpose NLP framework can facilitate the construction of PoS taggers for Gàidhlig and low-resource\nlanguages in general. Specifically, we explore the use of the spaCy library [9] to\ntrain two tagger models directly on the ARCOSG corpus with minimal configuration or preprocessing employed. Two levels of tag granularity are tested, aiming\nto assess the trade-off between simplicity and performance in a low-resource\nmorphologically complex setting.\nThe main contribution of this study is a reproducible lightweight Gàidhlig\nPoS tagging architecture, using off-the-shelf NLP tools and compatible with the\nspaCy software ecosystem, providing a competitive baseline for future research\nin Gàidhlig and other low-resource NLP settings. In contrast to the dozens of\nvaried PoS tagging systems available for widely used languages such as English\n(see [4] for a survey), to our knowledge only two taggers have been previously\ndescribed for Gàidhlig (see Section 2.2). We now provide now a third tagger, the\nfirst to use spaCy, making our code publicly available.\n\n2\n\nBackground\n\nNatural Language Processing is a very important part of human computer interactions. NLP techniques are used in everyday situations, such as spell checkers\nand speech recognition tools. The study of n-grams and statistical language\nmodelling represented a major advancement in NLP: text corpora can be used\nto observe patterns and establish word sequence probabilities, which then can\nbe used to model the internal logic of a given language [3].\n2.1\n\nGàidhlig\n\nGàidhlig is an Insular Celtic language indigenous to the British Isles, primarily\nspoken in the Scottish Highlands and Western Isles. Despite its widespread former use, and its great historical and cultural significance, the number of speakers\nhas declined in recent centuries, owing to a combination of historical suppression and broader political and social processes that have favoured the widespread\nadoption of English, resulting in its classification as an endangered language [11].\nMany revitalisation efforts have been made in response, including the development of digital and NLP-based tools in recent years. These could prove crucial\nin supporting language learning, improving accessibility and representation in\nonline spaces, and importantly helping the digitisation of Gàidhlig [20]. A brief\n\n\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n\n3\n\noverview of the history and current status of Gàidhlig can be found in a recent\nstudy by Barclay [23].\nGàidhlig presents several key challenges in the development of NLP tools,\ntypical of many low-resource languages. The existing corpora are very limited\nin size and availability. One primary and invaluable resource is the Annotated\nReference Corpus of Scottish Gaelic (ARCOSG), described in [17]. In contrast\nto high resource languages, Gàidhlig has a much smaller digital presence overall,\nmaking the development of robust NLP tools significantly more difficult [15].\nIn addition, Gàidhlig is a morphologically complex language. Grammatically\nimportant information is encoded into word inflections, mutational processes,\nand syntactic constructions [16,24]. NLP tasks are more difficult in such languages due to the added complexity of finding the lemma of words, which is\noften an important first processing step [28]. Additionally, morphologically rich\nlanguages have a much higher variety in their surface word forms, meaning that\na single lexical ‘word’ will have many variant forms occurring in texts [26]. The\nincreased variety will make each individual form less likely to occur in any given\ntraining corpus, resulting in a higher number of out of vocabulary (OOV) words\n– words that appear in a given text that have not been encountered before.\nThus, a language model might have more difficulty generalising effectively [13].\nThis problem is amplified in low-resource contexts owing to the already limited occurrences of these variant forms in the more restricted annotated corpora\n[13,22].\n2.2\n\nRelated work\n\nDespite these challenges, in recent years efforts have been made to develop computational resources for Gàidhlig. These include the creation of a dependency\ntreebank [1], which focused on capturing the morphological complexity and key\ngrammatical features of Gàidhlig. Projects on PoS taggers, linguistic analysis,\nand speech recognition applications have contributed to expanding the range\nof digital tools available for the language [19,2,7,14]. However, many existing\nlow-resource NLP systems remain highly specialised, typically designed for a\nsingle task. They rely on custom architectures with rule-based components or\nlanguage-specific feature-engineering to achieve high results.\nThe first tagger available for Gàidhlig, described by Lamb et al. [18], uses\nan ensemble-based Brill model, incorporating both stochastic and rule-based\nmethods, organised around a backoff mechanism. Following statistical tagging,\ncorrective rules are applied to increase the accuracy of the final tags. The only\nother prior tagger for Gàidhlig, developed by Boizou et al. [2] as part of the\nGaelic Linguistic Analyser (GLA) project, is based on the scikit-learn toolkit\nand uses additional training data in addition to ARCOSG. This tagger uses\nadditional features to capture grammatical structure, including prefixes and suffixes, sentence position and collocated word forms. Although both systems are\neffective, approaches requiring explicit linguistic expertise and language-specific\nfeature-engineering can increase system complexity and require greater development effort while reducing adaptability.\n\n\f4\n\nPéter Stefán et al.\n\nThese issues reflect a common pattern in low-resource NLP, where the lack of\nlarge annotated corpora leads to reliance on heavily engineered pipelines, transfer learning and multilingual pre-training approaches [8]. Such approaches can\nintroduce considerable complexity and dependence on external resources, and\nmay introduce a bias towards structures appearing any high-resource language\nmaterials used. In the case of Gàidhlig, this can be detrimental due to structural\nand morphological differences between the languages. For example, when using\ntransfer learning, the characteristic Verb-Subject-Object (VSO) word order of\nGàidhlig may not be adequately reflected in NLP resources developed primarily\nfor SVO (Subject-Verb-Object) languages such as English.\nTaking a new approach, this study investigates using a modern, lightweight,\ngeneral-purpose NLP pipeline using spaCy, trained directly on the ARCOSG\ncorpus with minimal pre-processing and configuration. This allows us to investigate how off-the-shelf NLP tools can be adopted for Gàidhlig PoS tagging. Our\nstudy establishes a baseline for comparison with more specialised systems, with\nthe main advantages being the ease of implementation and reduced system complexity. These characteristics make the approach potentially transferable across\nother low-resource languages.\n\n3\n\nAim\n\nDespite recent progress, Gàidhlig NLP remains relatively underdeveloped and\nis constrained by the limited annotated data and tools available. This paper\nexplores the effectiveness of Gàidhlig PoS tagging in a low-resource setting, focusing on tools previously unexplored in Gàidhlig research. We propose the use\nof the spaCy architecture for efficient model development, with minimal preprocessing, and limited hyperparameter tuning. The aim is to investigate whether\nsuch a lightweight NLP architecture can provide a useful baseline for future\ndevelopment in Gàidhlig language processing.\n\n4\n\nMethodology\n\nExisting work on Gàidhlig PoS tagging has focused on statistical taggers, developing specialised models with curated resources [20,2]. While these contributions\nhave significantly advanced the computational resources available for Gàidhlig,\nthey may be characterised as custom-built systems. Here, by contrast, we evaluate a general-purpose PoS tagging framework using off-the shelf components.\nLeveraging the modern NLP toolkit spaCy [9] remains unexplored for Gàidhlig.\nThis leaves open the question of whether lightweight, general-purpose pipelines\ncould be used as a baseline system for Gàidhlig PoS tagging and other downstream NLP tasks.\nTo address this gap, this study presents the first evaluation of a spaCy-based\npart-of-speech tagging pipeline for Gàidhlig. We propose two models trained\non the ARCOSG corpus with different levels of PoS tag granularity for wider\nevaluation. Both are implemented with very limited tuning, aiming to assess\n\n\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n\n5\n\nthe baseline performance of lightweight models for low-resource PoS tagging.\nThe results are reproducible, providing a useful basis for future Gàidhlig NLP\nresearch.\n4.1\n\nspaCy and the Pipeline Architecture\n\nAs the core NLP framework, we used spaCy [9], an open-source library for\nindustrial-strength NLP. The library provides a modular pipeline architecture\nthat supports a tokeniser, parser, tagger and other components for further downstream NLP tasks. The creators of spaCy also provide a number of pre-trained\nmodels and pipelines, mostly for high-resource languages. Here, spaCy was selected owing to its modular architecture and existing NLP utilities, which reduced and simplified implementation, while allowing for language-specific customisation. Rather than requiring individual implementations of tokenisation,\nfeature extraction, training, inference, and evaluation, these are provided through\na cohesive framework. The proposed pipeline is illustrated in Figure 1. Consequently, the implementation mainly focused on configuring an existing framework, assembling pipeline components and preparing language specific resources,\nrather than developing task-specific algorithms from scratch. This lowers the\ntechnical barrier for future Gàidhlig NLP research and simplifying the development of additional language technologies.\nThis approach is highly adaptable, so future work can build upon and extend\nthe proposed system requiring only the development of additional components,\nsuch as a lemmatiser, dependency parser or other language tools. This enables\nfuture Gàidhlig NLP research to build incrementally upon the proposed system,\ninstead of developing independent task-specific implementations, avoiding the\nneed to redesign the overall architecture for each new project.\n\nFig. 1. Overview of the Gàidhlig PoS tagging pipeline.\n\nMost importantly, the spaCy framework provides a lang module containing\nlanguage-specific information for the tokeniser. Thanks to the recent work of\nresearchers at Cornell College [5], the Gàidhlig module now includes a list of stopwords and tokeniser exceptions. The tokeniser also uses a rule set shared across\nmost languages as a default in spaCy1 . In addition to these built-in resources, the\nproposed pipeline used some auxiliary rules for punctuation handling required\nto ensure compatibility with the annotation conventions used in ARCOSG.\n1\n\nGàidhlig language folder for spaCy: https://github.com/explosion/spaCy/tree/\nmaster/spacy/lang/gd\n\n\f6\n\nPéter Stefán et al.\n\nThe pipeline was designed to be lightweight to reflect the low-resource conditions of Gàidhlig. Computationally expensive architectures, such as transformerbased models were excluded from consideration, since they typically require\nmuch larger training corpora for effective performance. In contrast, this study\nfocuses on a compact supervised pipeline that can be trained from the annotated ARCOSG corpus, with minimal pre-processing and limited computational\noverhead, while still capturing the rich morphology of Gàidhlig.\nThe proposed pipeline comprises three main stages: tokenisation of the raw\ntext, contextual feature extraction using Tok2Vec, and PoS prediction using\nspaCy’s tagger component. Each stage is described below.\nTok2Vec2 is the first trainable component of the pipeline. It converts each\nword from the raw input text into a numerical representation which captures\nboth the word itself and its surrounding context. These representation are based\non features such as word shape and the normalised form (a standardised form\nof the word used by spaCy, similar to the lemma). This helps the model encode\nimportant morphological information. The representations are then used as input\nby the tagger, which predicts the most likely PoS label for each token.\nThis architecture of spaCy is well suited to the morphological complexity\nof Gàidhlig. Grammatical information is learned from subword structures, such\nas affixes and other orthographic features, during the training process. This\nallows the model to generalise to previously unseen tokens – with low-resource\nlanguages, it is common to encounter words and surface forms that did not\nappear in the training data [22]. PoS predictions are also heavily affected by\nsyntactic context, making the contextual token representations from the Tok2Vec\ncomponent especially important in such low-resource settings, where the corpus\nmay otherwise contain insufficient examples [8].\nThe context encoding component only considers local contextual information.\nThis design prioritises vocabulary interactions nearer to the token, where PoS tag\ndistinctions are resolved through the immediate context and the morphological\ncues discussed above. As a result, the model learns through the relationship\nbetween context and morphology, rather than the meaning of the entire sentence\nor text. This also reduces model complexity, making the approach more suitable\nfor the limited amount of training data available.\nWe use no pre-trained vectors or external embeddings; the model must rely\npurely on gold-standard supervised labels from the annotated corpus.\nGiven the limited training data, this lightweight pipeline architecture focuses\non local contextual and morphological representations during training. This helps\nefficient learning in a low-resource context, while maintaining attention on orthographic and syntactic indicators that are relevant for PoS tagging [10].\n\n2\n\nTok2Vec documentation is available on the spaCy website: https://spacy.io/api/\ntok2vec\n\n\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n\n4.2\n\n7\n\nData\n\nThe entire ARCOSG corpus [17] was used for training and evaluation in this\nstudy. An 80-10-10 split was applied for training, validation and testing. Following the approach of prior studies [20] and [2], we use both a fine-grained (242 or\n246 items) and a coarse-grained (40 or 41 items) tag set for training and evaluation, making the comparison between previous work and our proposed models\nsimpler. The coarse-grained tags simplify some linguistic distinctions by grouping together a set of similar fine-grained tags. We opted for using all available\ndata to maximise the training and test sets; since no pre-training is done, the\nmodel is solely trained through supervised learning on the annotated corpus.\n4.3\n\nPreprocessing\n\nConsistent with the aim of the study, pre-processing was intentionally kept minimal in order to evaluate the effectiveness of a lightweight NLP pipeline using a\nspaCy-based structure in a low-resource context. No stemming or linguistic simplification was applied, to retain as much morphological information as possible,\nwhich is essential for morphologically complex PoS tagging [26,13].\nThe preprocessing focused on tokenisation consistency and compatibility with\nthe ARCOSG annotation system, while preserving orthographic and morphological structure. This minimal pre-processing was intended to keep the experiments\nreproducible and reduce over-dependence on language-specific modelling.\n\n5\n\nResults\n\nBoth tagging models were evaluated using a 10% randomly selected sample from\nARCOSG (the unseen test data set). The main metric used to measure performance is tagging accuracy, meaning the percentage of PoS tags that were\ncorrectly predicted by the model. The model using the larger ARCOSG tagset\nachieved an accuracy of 88.60%. The other one, trained on the reduced set of\ntags, performed with 93.7% accuracy. These results are comparable with the accuracy of previously reported Gàidhlig PoS taggers. Table 1 presents the model\narchitecture of both models and their performance.\n\nTagset Type\nTagset Size\nTraining Tokens\nVocabulary Size\nPOS Tag Accuracy\n\nFull Tagset Model\nFine-grained PoS tags\n215\n70,890\n7160\n88.60%\n\nReduced Tagset Model\nCoarse PoS tags\n40\n69,522\n7114\n93.70%\n\nTable 1. Performance and training set comparison between the full and reduced ARCOSG tagset models.\n\n\f8\n\nPéter Stefán et al.\n\nTwo models were evaluated, using the coarse- and the fine-grained tagset\nrespectively. The full tagset has 215 tags, the reduced set 40 coarse-grained\ntags. The training corpus contains 70,890 total words, with a vocabulary of 7160\nunique entries; this is slightly smaller for the reduced set. The spaCy architecture\neven gives a warning when the training set is validated, indicating that the\ncurrent number of examples may not be sufficient for proper training.\nSome minor tokenisation inconsistencies remained, as ∼0.4% of the tokens are\nlabelled “misaligned”. However, this was expected, as only the stop words and\nbasic tokenisation exceptions were applied from the Gàidhlig language defaults\nin the spaCy framework.\n\n6\n\nDiscussion\n\nBoth models show encouraging performance, with the full fine-grained model\nachieving results comparable to earlier specialised systems of Lamb et al. [19]\nand Boizou et al. [2]. The results in Table 2 suggest that a lightweight spaCybased pipeline can achieve competitive performance in low-resource settings for\nmorphologically complex languages with minimal language-specific engineering.\nThe difference in performance between the full and reduced tagsets highlights\nthe morphological complexity of Gàidhlig, as the full tagset requires the model to\ndistinguish between much finer grammatical categories. The reduced set collapses\nlinguistically subtle and context dependent distinctions making the classification\ntask substantially simpler. In the low-resource setting used in these experiments,\nmany of the 215 fine-grained tags appear infrequently: this further increases the\ndifficulty of this task. Without pre-training or external word embeddings, the\nmodel must solely rely on corpus examples, which limits its ability to learn the\nmore complex PoS tags.\n\nStudy\n\nTagset Type\n\nTagset Size\n\nDanso & Lamb (2014) Fine-grained 242 tags (PAROLE tagset)\nLamb et al. (2016)\nFine-grained 246 tags\nLamb et al. (2016)\nCoarse-grained 41 tags\nBoizou & Lamb (2020) Fine-grained 246 tags\nBoizou & Lamb (2020) Coarse-grained 41 tags\nOur model\nFine-grained 246 tags\nOur model\nCoarse-grained 40 tags\n\nAccuracy\n76.6\n84%\n92%\n90.7%\n94.7%\n88.6%\n93.7%\n\nTable 2. Performance comparison of previous and proposed models. Lamb et al.[19]\nprovided additional evaluation on the same model developed by Danso & Lamb[6].\n\nThese findings are significant given the simplicity of the implementation and\nthe model architecture. The application of the spaCy framework appears to\nperform effectively with minimal configuration. The models are able to learn\n\n\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n\n9\n\nmorphological patterns, suggesting that the Tok2Vec architecture can generalise\nreasonably well in low-resource settings through limited local context, subword\nand orthographic information.\nThe design decision to preserve morphological information during preprocessing is also supported by these results. As noted, no stemming or lemmatisation was applied, allowing the model to learn directly from the various surface\nforms. The relatively strong performance, even with the full tagset, could suggest that preserving the surface features is just as beneficial as more customised\napproaches commonly used in low-resource tagging tasks.\nThese experiments were designed to be reproducible, as the default settings\nwere applied in most cases. A future study could investigate customisation of\nthe implementation, to investigate the best pipeline structure for Gàidhlig using\nspaCy.\nOur results suggest that a lightweight, general-purpose NLP pipeline can\nprovide a practical and reproducible baseline for Gàidhlig PoS tagging. The\nuse of spaCy significantly reduced the implementation difficulty of the proposed\npipelines. The use of two sets of tags with differing granularity highlights the\nimportance of tokenisation quality when addressing the complex grammatical\nstructures in Gàidhlig.\nFor Gàidhlig and other languages with complex morphology and limited resources, these results are promising for future experiments with similar pipelines\nusing the spaCy architecture.\n\n7\n\nFurther Work\n\nAs this research is ongoing, we hope to implement similar pipelines using spaCy\nwith more sophisticated architectures. Ideas include introducing pre-training via\nmultilingual models such as XLM-RoBERTA3 . This would allow evaluation of\nwhether multilingual models provide a better starting state. Some models, such\nas XLM-RoBERTA, also include Gàidhlig texts in their training data, perhaps\nmaking them a better choice. On the other hand, this approach could also introduce other limitations, such as a bias towards the grammatical structure\nof high-resource languages in the training set leading to negative transfer [27].\nFuture work could also investigate whether using cross-lingual transfer would\nprovide any improvements for Gàidhlig models over the lightweight architecture\nproposed here.\nUsing additional data sources for unsupervised learning could be employed\nto increase the learning potential of future models. Given the limited available\nannotated corpora, this approach provides a more direct way of addressing the\nissue. Unlabelled Gàidhlig texts could be used for self-supervised pre-training,\nproviding the models with further examples to learn lexical and morphological\npatterns before tuning them with the annotated data. Data augmentation techniques, such as synthetic sentence generation, paraphrasing, morphology-aware\n3\n\nhttps://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model doc/xlm-roberta\n\n\f10\n\nPéter Stefán et al.\n\ntransformations, and synonym replacement, could also increase the diversity in\nthe training set. These approaches, in the case of a language with complex morphology, may lead to improved generalisation [25].\nAdditional analysis of the model predictions could pinpoint which tags are\nthe most frequently misclassified and which grammatical distinctions are the\nmost challenging. The significant performance difference shown between the full\nand reduced tagsets suggests that most errors may occur because of fine-grained\nhighly specialised grammatical categories. Detailed error analysis could provide\nvaluable insight into whether errors primarily arise between related fine-grained\ncategories or broader syntactic ones. Understanding the confusion patterns of\nthe models could guide future improvements in annotation schemes, model architectures, and preprocessing strategies [21].\n\n8\n\nConclusion\n\nThis paper presents our evaluation of the effectiveness of lightweight PoS tagging pipelines based on the spaCy architecture for Gàidhlig using the ARCOSG\ncorpus. The goal was to assess whether a model with minimal preprocessing and\nconfiguration could perform as well as the more heavily engineered approaches\ncommon in low-resource NLP.\nThe results show that both models achieved relatively strong performance.\nThe first model using the fine-grained 215 tagset achieved 88.6%, while the\nreduced coarse-grained 40-tag model had an accuracy of 93.7%. These indicate\nthat the proposed pipeline using the spaCy presets in most cases is still capable\nof learning complex morphological and contextual representations for Gàidhlig\ntags, despite the limited data available for training. The difference in accuracy\nbetween the two models is due to the variation in tag granularity, highlighting\nthe need for additional information to address subtle grammatical distinctions.\nOverall, this work contributes a reproducible baseline for Gàidhlig PoS tagging, using the spaCy architecture with minimal configuration needed, which is a\nnew approach for the language. The results are promising as they are comparable\nin accuracy to previous work reported in the literature, which employed more\ncomplex architectures and specific configuration for Gàidhlig grammatical structures. As new linguistic resources become available for Gàidhlig, baseline systems\nsuch as ours can provide a foundation for future research into more advanced\nNLP solutions and downstream language technologies to support the digital\npresence of this living, culturally important but under-resourced language.\n\nCode Availability Statement\nAll code from this research is made publicly available in the GitHub repository at:\nhttps://github.com/Speter011/A-Modular-Language-Model-for-Scottish-Gaelic.\n\n\fA Modular Gàidhlig PoS Tagger\n\n11\n\nReferences\n1. Colin Batchelor. Universal dependencies for Scottish Gaelic: syntax. In Proceedings\nof the Celtic Language Technology Workshop, pages 7–15, 2019.\n2. Loı̈c Boizou and William Lamb. An online linguistic analyser for Scottish Gaelic.\nIn Human Language Technologies – The Baltic Perspective, pages 119–122. IOS\nPress, 2020.\n3. Ciprian Chelba. Statistical Language Modeling, pages 74–104. Wiley-Blackwell,\nJohn Wiley & Sons Ltd, John Wiley & Sons Ltd, The Atrium, Southern Gate,\nChichester, West Sussex, PO19 8SQ United Kingdom, 2010. Parts of this chapter\nappeared in Computer Speech & Language, vol. 14, no. 4, pages 283-332, October\n2000, and are used with the permission of Elsevier Limited.\n4. Alebachew Chiche and Betselot Yitagesu. Part of speech tagging: a systematic\nreview of deep learning and machine learning approaches. Journal of Big Data,\n9(1):10, 2022.\n5. Scottish Gaelic Collab. Gaelic lemmatizer (spaCy-compatible NLP pipeline). https:\n//github.com/Scottish-Gaelic-Collab/lemmatizer gaelic, 2025. Accessed: 2026-0611.\n6. Samuel Danso and William Lamb. Developing an automatic part-of-speech tagger\nfor Scottish Gaelic. In John Judge, Teresa Lynn, Monica Ward, and Oisı́n Ruairı́\nÓ Brian, editors, Proceedings of the Celtic Technology Workshop (CLTW 2014):\nA Workshop of the 25th International Conference on Computational Linguistics\n(COLING 2014), pages 1–5, Dublin, Ireland, August 2014.\n7. Lucy Evans, William Lamb, Mark Sinclair, and Beatrice Alex. Developing automatic speech recognition for Scottish Gaelic. In Proceedings of the 4th Celtic\nlanguage technology workshop within LREC2022, pages 110–120, 2022.\n8. Michael A. Hedderich, Lukas Lange, Heike Adel, Jannik Strötgen, and Dietrich Klakow. A survey on recent approaches for natural language processing in\nlow-resource scenarios. In Kristina Toutanova, Anna Rumshisky, Luke Zettlemoyer, Dilek Hakkani-Tur, Iz Beltagy, Steven Bethard, Ryan Cotterell, Tanmoy\nChakraborty, and Yichao Zhou, editors, Proceedings of the 2021 Conference of the\nNorth American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies, pages 2545–2568, Online, June 2021. Association for\nComputational Linguistics.\n9. Matthew Honnibal, Ines Montani, Sofie Van Landeghem, and Adriane Boyd.\nspaCy: Industrial-strength natural language processing in Python, 2020. https:\n//doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1212303.\n10. Daniel Jurafsky and James H. Martin. Speech and Language Processing: An Introduction to Natural Language Processing, Computational Linguistics, and Speech\nRecognition, with Language Models. Stanford University (online manuscript released January 6, 2026), 3rd edition, 2026. https://web.stanford.edu/∼jurafsky/\nslp3/.\n11. Anne Kandler, Roman Unger, and James Steele. Language shift, bilingualism and\nthe future of Britain’s Celtic languages. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal\nSociety B: Biological Sciences, 365(1559):3855–3864, 2010.\n12. Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, Tom Henighan, Tom B. Brown, Benjamin Chess,\nRewon Child, Scott Gray, Alec Radford, Jeffrey Wu, and Dario Amodei. Scaling\nlaws for neural language models, 2020. https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361.\n13. Irakli Kardava. A morphology-driven approach to NLP for a low-resource, highly\ncomplex language. Vietnam Journal of Computer Science, 1:19, 2025.\n\n\f12\n\nPéter Stefán et al.\n\n14. Ondřej Klejch, William Lamb, and Peter Bell. A practitioner’s guide to building ASR models for low-resource languages: A case study on Scottish Gaelic. In\nProceedings of Interspeech 2025, pages 728–732, 2025.\n15. András Kornai. Language death in the digital age. Language, 1(8), 2012.\n16. William Lamb. Scottish Gaelic: A comprehensive grammar. Routledge, 2024.\n17. William Lamb, Sharon Arbuthnot, Susanna Naismith, and Samuel Danso. Annotated reference corpus of Scottish Gaelic (ARCOSG), 1997–2020, 2020. https:\n//github.com/Gaelic-Algorithmic-Research-Group/ARCOSG.\n18. William Lamb and Samuel Danso. Developing an automatic part-of-speech tagger for Scottish Gaelic. In Proceedings of the First Celtic Language Technology\nWorkshop, pages 1–5, 2014.\n19. William Lamb, Samuel Danso, and Alistair Lawson. Evaluating a Gaelic Part-ofSpeech Tagger and Reference Corpus. Technical report, University of Edinburgh,\nEdinburgh, Scotland, 2016.\n20. William Lamb and Mark Sinclair. Developing Word Embedding Models for Scottish Gaelic. In Actes de la conférence conjointe JEP-TALN-RECITAL, volume 6,\npages 31–41, 2016.\n21. Kaihong Liu, Wendy Chapman, Rebecca Hwa, and Rebecca S Crowley. Heuristic\nsample selection to minimize reference standard training set for a part-of-speech\ntagger. Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 14(5):641–650,\n2007.\n22. Alexandre Magueresse, Vincent Carles, and Evan Heetderks. Low-resource languages: A review of past work and future challenges, 2020. https://arxiv.org/abs/\n2006.07264.\n23. Peter J Barclay. A Rule-Based Computational Model for Gàidhlig Morphology. In\nProceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence, volume 1, pages 683–693. SCITEPRESS – Science and Technology Publications, Lda., March 2026.\n24. Susan Ross. The Standardisation of Scottish Gaelic Orthography 1750–2007. PhD\nthesis, University of Glasgow, 2016. https://theses.gla.ac.uk/7403/.\n25. Gözde Gül Şahin and Mark Steedman. Data augmentation via dependency tree\nmorphing for low-resource languages. In Proceedings of the 2018 Conference on\nEmpirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 5004–5009, 2018.\n26. Reut Tsarfaty, Djamé Seddah, Sandra Kübler, and Joakim Nivre. Parsing morphologically rich languages: Introduction to the special issue. Computational linguistics, 39(1):15–22, 2013.\n27. Zirui Wang, Zachary C. Lipton, and Yulia Tsvetkov. On negative interference in\nmultilingual models: Findings and a meta-learning treatment. In Bonnie Webber,\nTrevor Cohn, Yulan He, and Yang Liu, editors, Proceedings of the 2020 Conference\non Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP), pages 4438–4450.\nAssociation for Computational Linguistics, November 2020. https://aclanthology.\norg/2020.emnlp-main.359/.\n28. Marion Weller-Di Marco and Alexander Fraser. Analyzing the understanding of\nmorphologically complex words in large language models. In Proceedings of the\n2024 Joint International Conference on Computational Linguistics, Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC-COLING 2024), pages 1009–1020, 2024.\n\n\f", "numLines": 571, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 571}}}
